<<~ ahu slot=`#/1-verify-round-one-s-load-bearing-claims`>>

!! 1. VERIFY — round one's load-bearing claims

!!! 1.1 Codex plan: per-5h message estimates — HOLDS (numbers) · OVERSTATED (applies to this account)
* SOURCED https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/pricing (developers.openai.com/codex/pricing 308-redirects here): "The
estimates below show local messages per five-hour period for Plus and Standard Business": Astra 5–45, GPT-6.1 Sol
15–160, GPT-6.1 Terra 35–285, ''GPT-6 Sol 15–150'' (round one omitted it), Luna 350–3,000. "These estimates are not
fixed message limits." "Weekly limits may also apply." "ChatGPT Work and Codex share usage."
* SOURCED, same page, credit rates per 1M tokens, input / cached / output: **GPT-6.1 Sol 50 / 2.5 / 250 · GPT-6 Sol
50 / 5 / 250** · GPT-6.1 Terra 25 / 1.25 / 125 · GPT-6 Luna 2.5 / 0.25 / 12.5 · Astra 250 / 25 / 1,250. API prices
follow the same ratios (https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/pricing: gpt-6-sol cached $0.20 vs gpt-6.1-sol $0.10).
* SOURCED: "Fast mode uses 2x the Standard rate where available, and Ultrafast mode uses 6x." The page's table shows Fast
at 2.5× of included usage. Ultrafast is listed only for "Pro $500 and eligible Enterprise/Edu plans".
* ''OVERSTATED'': round one framed the pool as "five-hour and weekly windows". MEASURED from this machine's rollouts
(`payload.rate_limits` in `~/.codex/sessions/2026/10/2*/rollout-*.jsonl`): `"limit_id":"codex"`,
`"primary":{"window_minutes":10080,...}`, `"secondary":null`, `"plan_type":"team"`. One weekly window, with no
five-hour window on this account now. So the per-5h estimates convert to a weekly budget here; they do not set a 5h
ceiling.

!!! 1.2 Copilot legacy multipliers — HOLDS · "can legacy annual select the cheap ones" — NOT ESTABLISHED by docs
* SOURCED https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/reference/copilot-billing/request-based-billing-legacy/model-multipliers-for-annual-plans:
GPT-5.3-Codex "6", GPT-5.1-Codex-Mini "0.33", GPT-5.1-Codex "3", Claude Haiku 4.5 "0.33", MAI-Code-1.1-Flash "0.25".
"Users on legacy annual Copilot plans will not receive access to new models and features." The page names no model
that a legacy user can or cannot select.
* SOURCED https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/reference/copilot-billing/request-based-billing-legacy/copilot-requests:
"Each prompt to Copilot CLI uses ''one premium request'' with the default model", multiplied by the model's rate;
code review: "''13 premium requests''"; "Premium request counters reset on the 1st of each month at 00:00:00 UTC";
"$0.04/request" overage.
* MEASURED, crew record (feedback_mixed_model_swarm, 2026-10-16): on THIS account `claude-haiku-4.5` and `gpt-5-mini`
(0.33) and `mai-code-1.1-flash` (0.25) open, and "the gpt-5.1-codex line is NOT open". So GPT-5.1-Codex-Mini at 0.33
holds as a price but not as a choice here. Haiku 4.5 at 0.33 stands as the cheap Copilot seat (18× cheaper than
GPT-5.3-Codex per prompt). A 2026-10-19 note records Gemini/MAI-Code models answering "model unavailable". So re-probe
MAI-Flash before relying on it.

!!! 1.3 Anthropic per-model pricing — HOLDS
* SOURCED https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing: Opus 5.5 "$4 / MTok | $5 | $8 | $0.20 | $20";
Sonnet 5.5 "$2 | $2.50 | $4 | $0.10 | $10"; Haiku 5.5 "(for prompts up to 100,000 tokens) $0.10 | $0.125 | $0.20 |
$0.01 | $0.50", over 100k "$0.50 | $0.625 | $1 | $0.05 | $2.50"; Fable 5.1 "$10 | $12.50 | $20 | $0.25 | $50". Footnote:
"Cache hits and refreshes on Claude Opus 5.5 and Claude Sonnet 5.5 are priced at 0.05x the base input price."
"A request's prompt length counts all of its input tokens, including cache reads and cache writes" (Haiku tier).

!!! 1.4 Claude Code plan metering — HOLDS, with one gap closed
* SOURCED https://support.claude.com/en/articles/11145838: "Both Pro and Max plans have a five-hour session limit and a
weekly limit"; Max has "a separate weekly limit for Fable"; "limits are shared across Claude and Claude Code".
* SOURCED https://support.claude.com/en/articles/14552983: Opus "uses meaningfully more of your quota"; "Opus costs
several times more per turn than Sonnet, and Sonnet more than Haiku"; "Pro tip: plan with Opus, execute with Sonnet."
It gives no numeric ratio. Round one's "cannot be derived" holds for the ratio.
* ''Gap closed (SOURCED https://code.claude.com/docs/en/sub-agents)'': a subagent "sends its own requests, which count
toward the same [usage limits] as your main conversation." So subagent seats draw the same Max pool as the controller.
* MEASURED: this account runs `subscriptionType: max`, `rateLimitTier: default_claude_max_20x`, Claude Code 2.1.312.

!!! 1.5 Prompt caching in Claude Code — round one REASONED it; it is now SOURCED and MEASURED
* SOURCED https://code.claude.com/docs/en/prompt-caching: on a subscription, "Main conversation: One hour"; "Everything
else [subagents, workflows, forks…]: Five minutes". "Subagents fall outside the main-conversation TTL bucket, so they
get five minutes even on a subscription until you choose a longer one." Controls: "`subagentPromptCacheTtl` setting,
or the `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_PROMPT_CACHE_TTL` environment variable" (`5m`|`1h`, v2.1.242+); also a per-agent
`experimental.cacheTtl` frontmatter (v2.1.248+). "Two sessions in different directories therefore build different
prefixes and miss each other's cache." Effort changes keep the cache on Opus/Sonnet/Haiku 5.5. A model switch does not.
* MEASURED (transcripts since 10-26): main session cache writes are 100% `ephemeral_1h`; subagent writes are 100%
`ephemeral_5m`. See §2.3 for what that costs.

!!! 1.6 Terra's positioning — round one HOLDS, and it understated the point
* SOURCED https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-6.1-terra: "A fast, efficient model for visual reasoning,
precise computer use, and clear, natural writing." Coding is not mentioned.
* SOURCED https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/models (Codex models page): Terra "A fast, efficient model for clear writing,
visual understanding, and computer use", recommended for writing, images/screens and in-app tasks. Sol 6.1: "Consider
GPT-6.1 Sol for work across code, apps, and documents when cost matters." Luna: "focused, high-volume tasks, including
summarization, extraction, and focused coding"; "Choose Luna … when you know what a good result looks like". Effort:
"Start with High for Luna or Light for Astra." The page's only review example runs &#96;codex exec -m gpt-6.1-sol "Review
the current changes"&#96;.
* MEASURED (`~/.codex/models_cache.json`, fetched 14:13Z today): gpt-6.1-terra "GPT-6.1 Terra Codex model.", default
effort ''low''; gpt-6.1-sol "Latest workhorse model for coding and everyday work.", default medium, nux "Maximize usage
with GPT-6.1 Sol"; gpt-6-sol "Previous generation workhorse model."; gpt-6-luna "Fast and affordable model for easier
tasks."; gpt-reserve (hidden) "Fast and affordable agentic coding model."

!!! 1.7 "Fresh eyes beat a second family" — HOLDS, but round one missed its most useful finding
* SOURCED https://stephens.page/blog/fresh-eyes-beat-a-second-family/paper/ (J. Stephens, 2026-10-12; Opus 5.5 vs
GPT-6.1 Sol xhigh; 183+24 sessions). A fresh same-model reviewer "matched or beat the other family's catches at every
checkpoint"; "where the second look is placed matters more than who takes it."
* The one checkpoint that paid off is the ''second look after the spec-level review'': Codex found real missed bugs in
16/30 features and fresh Claude in 19/30 (bar 6), and "the two families' findings were largely disjoint." Plan-check:
only fresh Claude at MAX effort passed (8/10 stops right). Codex stopped 15 Claude plans, only 5 of them correctly.
Authoring agents declined 2 of 2 correct escaped-bug flags.
* Limits (stated by the paper): Claude graders, one run per cell, small n, one system.
* REASONED reading for this crew: a cross-lineage read pays at the slice-level second look, because it finds disjoint
bugs. Most of the value comes from the read being fresh and well placed, not from the family. The "authors declined
correct flags" result supports refinement 5 (readers route straight to the builder, the controller rules on forks).
It also argues for the controller, not the builder, closing HIGH findings.

!!! 1.8 Codex `/usage` — WRONG as cited
* SOURCED https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/pricing: "If you want to see your remaining limits during an active Codex CLI
session, you can use `/status`." The CLI page (https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/codex/cli) shows no `/usage`. `/usage`
belongs to Claude Code (prompt-caching page: "For a per-session summary, run `/usage`").

