<<~ ahu slot=`#/2-gaps-filled`>>

!! 2. GAPS — filled

!!! 2.1 Claude Code subagent model selection (SOURCED https://code.claude.com/docs/en/sub-agents)
* Yes, a subagent can run Haiku 5.5. `model:` takes "`sonnet`, `opus`, `haiku`, `fable`, a full model ID … or
`inherit`". On an Agent tool call, "that parameter takes a model alias: `sonnet`, `opus`, `haiku`, or `fable`", and
"It applies on resume and follow-up messages". Precedence runs: per-call param > frontmatter >
`CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL` > main model. `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL_FORCE=1` pins every subagent.
* Effort per call: "Claude can pass `effort` per call for non-fork subagents (requires v2.1.292+)" (we run 2.1.312);
`low|medium|high|xhigh|max`. `CLAUDE_CODE_EFFORT_LEVEL` overrides both.
* Weighting: no published ratio (§1.4). REASONED: API price ratios Opus:Sonnet:Haiku = 40:20:1 on input and output
(Haiku ≤100k). "Several times more per turn" fits ~2× per token plus Opus's longer turns. Treat 2× as the floor.
* Haiku tier hazard (REASONED): a subagent here carries the Claude Code system prompt, tools, CLAUDE.md and the boot
.mem. After a few file reads it passes 100k, and every later turn bills at the >100k tier ($0.50 in / $2.50 out).
That is still 4× under Sonnet.

!!! 2.2 Reading the Codex pool from a script (MEASURED; mechanism not documented, so treat it as a field that can change)
* Every Codex rollout `token_count` event carries `rate_limits.primary.used_percent`, `window_minutes`, `resets_at`,
`plan_type`, `credits`. Read the first and last per rollout. spend.py does not yet read them. Five lines would add a
"pool %" column per lane.
* Last week's window (resets_at 2026-11-02 06:26Z), MEASURED by pairing each rollout's pool readings with its tokens ×
the credit table:
** The pool went ''5% → 100% between 2026-10-26 10:10Z and 2026-10-27 07:14Z'', about 21 hours of lane work.
** All of that draw was gpt-6-sol: ≈1,566 credit-eq, of which ''1,052 (68%) was cached input''. That gives ≈16.5
credits per 1%.
** k15-bonds alone took 57→89% in about 2 hours (82.5M cached tokens).
* A new window opened 2026-10-28 ≈11:24Z (resets 2026-11-04 11:24Z) before the old one's reset. The cause does not show
in the logs (operator "refreshed"/credits?). By 14:14Z it stood at 17%: ≈251 credit-eq (gpt-6-sol 217, Terra 23,
6.1-sol 10), ≈14.8 credits per 1%. The two windows agree within 10%. So **the plan meter tracks the published credit
rates**, and spend.py can price any seat in credits.
* Per-seat (10-28, MEASURED, 1% granularity, concurrent seats blur it):
** gpt-6-sol medium builder seats: 1–5% each.
** Terra medium seats: 0–1% each (≈23 credits over 6 seats).
** One gpt-6.1-sol high read: 1% (10.4 credits).
** REASONED: a ~1% Terra seat against a 3–5% gpt-6-sol seat compares unequal work. That is why E2 exists.
* REASONED: the pool is shared with the operator's own ChatGPT Work use (SOURCED §1.1), so a lane's % delta can include
his chats. Credits-from-tokens is the cleaner per-lane number. The % reading is the budget truth.

!!! 2.3 Where the Claude spend goes (MEASURED, transcripts since 2026-10-26, API-eq $; 5m writes priced at 1.25× input)
| Bucket | Seats | API-eq $ | Of which cache WRITES |h
| Controller (main session, Opus, 1h cache) | 1 | ≈79 | 19 |
| Opus subagents (hands-spine 12 seats $130, porch 1 $16, research 2 $3) | 15 | ≈149 | 57 |
| Sonnet subagents (Episkopoi + hands-spine readers) | 29 | ≈331 | ''229'' |
* Sonnet subagents made 3,408 calls. ''250 of them followed a 5–60 minute idle gap and re-wrote 83M tokens'' (≈$207
API-eq, 63% of Sonnet spend). Opus: 29 such rewrites, 7.8M (≈$39, 26%).
* The gap is the Episkopos waiting on a background Codex seat. The wait outlives the 5-minute TTL, so each wake pays a
full re-write at 1.25× input instead of a 0.05× read.
* REASONED projection with `subagentPromptCacheTtl: "1h"`: re-writes become reads (83M × $0.10 ≈ $8), and the remaining
≈8–10M of writes cost 1.6× more ($4 vs $2.50). Net Sonnet ≈$331 → ≈$150 (−55%), Opus ≈$149 → ≈$120. Unverified
against the plan meter. E1 measures it.
* Hands-spine, MEASURED: since 10-26 the lane holds ''12 Opus + 11 Sonnet seats'' (spend.py; all-time 16 Opus-5.5 + 7
older Opus + 1 Haiku-4.5). The brief's "one slice took 8 Opus and 10 Sonnet seats" sits inside that total. spend.py
cannot split by slice, so I cannot confirm the 8/10 split from tool logs.
* spend.py blind spots (MEASURED by reading it):
** The controller's own main session is not tallied (it globs only `subagents/agent-*.jsonl`).
** It does not split 5m from 1h writes.
** It prints raw tokens, not credits.
** Ephemeral Codex seats (all Luna reads to date) are invisible.

!!! 2.4 Effort as a cost lever, per role (REASONED on SOURCED controls)
* Codex: output tokens cost 10× input, and reasoning tokens count as output. Effort mostly moves output and turn count.
In this crew's seats, output was ~14% of gpt-6-sol credits and cached input 68%. **Context length per seat beats
effort as a lever**: shorter seats, a fresh seat per slice rather than one long resumed seat, and briefs that point at
files rather than paste them.
* Claude: per-call `effort` now exists. Episkopos (drives seats, runs suites) → `medium`. Readers → `high`. A
controller's named rulings → `max` (the plan-check result in §1.7 favoured max).

!!! 2.5 Stable brief prefixes (REASONED on §1.5)
* A Claude subagent's cache keys on system prompt + tools + CLAUDE.md + its opening message, per directory. Two seats
whose briefs share a long common head share that prefix only while warm, in the same cwd, within the TTL.
* Gains: put the stable crew text (fences, battery, hand-back shape) FIRST and identical; put the lane-specific part
LAST. Codex caches the same way (its cached-input line). A stable `AGENTS.md`-led head helps both.

