<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/3-yin-the-strongest-case-against-the-current-ros`>>

!! 3. YIN — the strongest case against the current roster

''Y1. Terra + Luna under-reviews, and possibly under-builds.''
* OpenAI never positions Terra for code (§1.6). Its local default effort is //low//. The Codex docs point code work at
Sol 6.1.
* The only public recall number for a Luna line (SOURCED https://hyper.ai/de/stories/61420e1b8b747e0a57a34e18041183d9,
Sept 2026, 50 public PRs, two-judge verification):
** GPT-5.6 Luna found 69 verified bugs against Astra's 92, at 74% precision against 96%.
** On security, "Luna identifizierte nur 9 von 24 Sicherheitslücken" (Astra 19).
* Launch benchmarks show GPT-6 Luna close to GPT-6 Sol on DeepSWE (66.6% vs 68.8%), but ''at max effort''. Its factual
error rate runs 7.6% vs 4.5% (SOURCED https://computingforgeeks.com/gpt-6-sol-luna-released-features-benchmarks/,
updated 2026-10-27).
* The crew runs Luna at medium, on a repo whose lanes are capability, custody, crypto and gate code: exactly the
security class Luna missed.
* A pairing of a builder not made for code and a reader weak on security leaves the Sonnet Episkopos as the only real
review. The controller's 2026-10-19 ruling already moved the deep read to the controller.

''Y2. Opus seats should not build at all.''
* 87% of Opus subagent spend sat in one lane's builder seats (§2.3).
* Anthropic's own guidance: "plan with Opus, execute with Sonnet."
* The controller already reads every chunk. An Opus builder pays Opus prices for work a Sonnet or Codex builder does
under the same review.
* The hands-spine balloon (8 seats, ~40 commits) came from scope creep, which refinement 2 now caps, not from model
weakness.

''Y3. The controller should run on Sonnet, with Opus called for named rulings.''
* The controller is the single largest Claude line after the Sonnet re-writes: ≈$79 API-eq in 2.5 days, 237M
cache-read tokens.
* Most controller turns read hand-backs, launch seats and run spend.py. That is Sonnet work.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/3-yang-what-survives`>>

!! 3′. YANG — what survives

* ''Y3 fails.'' The controller is the one seat where depth decides outcomes.
** The fresh-eyes paper's only passing plan-check ran fresh Claude at //max//, and authors declined correct flags. The
seat that rules on forks must be the strongest.
** The controller costs ≈14% of Claude spend. Halving it saves less than the cache fix saves on one lane.
** What survives from Y3: the controller hands mechanical turns (spend tallies, launches, greps) to a Haiku/Sonnet
helper. It does not downgrade itself.
* ''Y2 survives.'' Opus leaves the default builder slot. It stays available for a named cross-process-seam slice, with
the reason written in the brief.
* ''Y1 survives in part.''
** Terra stays the builder on trial. It costs half of Sol 6.1 per credit. Its seats so far drew ≈1% each, and the crew
reads every build twice anyway. E2 decides it within a week.
** Luna stays the first, every-slice reader, at ''high'' effort (OpenAI's own starting point). It never stands as the
sole substantive read of a slice touching caps, custody, crypto, gate or keyhive: those get Sol-6.1 high. In this
repo that is often, so "Sol only for named deep questions" becomes "Sol for named deep questions AND for every
security-class slice."
** Sol-6.1 replaces gpt-6-sol everywhere. Same credit price on input and output, half the cached rate, newer.
** The "speedy" Sol the operator saw was mostly gpt-6-sol plus 80M-token seats. Sol 6.1 in short, fresh seats should
not reproduce last week's 21-hour drain.

---

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/4-the-roster-ratcheted`>>

!! 4. THE ROSTER, ratcheted (one page)

Pools: ''Max-20x'' (Claude, 5h + weekly, subagents included) · ''Codex team, weekly'' (≈1,600 credit-eq/week MEASURED;
shared with ChatGPT Work) · ''Copilot legacy annual'' (300/month, resets 11-01 00:00Z, no rollover, ends at term).

| Role | First choice | Fallback | Effort | Pool | Cost reasoning (this crew's numbers) |h
| Controller | Opus 5.5 main session (1h cache already) | — (no downgrade); Fable only for a named ruling | high; `max` for named rulings | Max | ≈$79 API-eq/2.5 days, ≈14% of Claude spend; the seat that rules forks. Hand tallies and launches to helpers. |
| Episkopos (lane lead) | Sonnet 5.5 subagent, ''`subagentPromptCacheTtl: 1h`'' | Opus only if the lane is a named seam lane | medium | Max | 63% of Sonnet spend was 5m-cache re-writes after Codex waits; 1h TTL projects ≈−55% (E1). |
| Builder | Codex `gpt-6.1-terra` medium (trial) | `gpt-6.1-sol` ''low/medium'' in a fresh seat; then Sonnet 5.5 subagent | medium | Codex | Terra 25/1.25/125 credits vs Sol-6.1 50/2.5/250. Keep seats short: cached re-reads were 68% of last week's draw. Opus builders retired as default (87% of Opus seat spend). |
| Reader (every slice) | Codex `gpt-6-luna` ''high'', read-only, NON-ephemeral | Copilot Haiku 4.5 (0.33×) | high | Codex | Luna ≈1/10 of Terra per credit. Recall weak on security (§3), so it is first pass, never the sole read on security-class slices. |
| Deep reader | Codex `gpt-6.1-sol` high: named deep question ''or any caps/custody/crypto/gate/keyhive slice'' | Opus 5.5 subagent, fresh, high | high | Codex / Max | One Sol-6.1 high read ≈10 credits ≈0.6% of the week (MEASURED). Cheap next to an escaped security bug. |
| Researcher | Codex `gpt-6.1-sol` medium `--search` (web works today) | Opus 5.5 subagent with WebSearch/WebFetch (this seat) | medium | Codex / Max | Last week's 18 research Sol seats drew ≈0–1% each. Research is cheap; depth matters more. Opus for ratchet rounds like this one. |
| Sweeper (prose, naming, evergreen) | Claude ''Haiku 5.5'' subagent (`model: haiku`) for mechanical passes | Luna high | low–medium | Max | ≈1/20 of Sonnet per token (1/4 past 100k). Witness scripts gate meaning; the controller reads the diff. |
| Batch Aftermath | Copilot ''Haiku 4.5'' (0.33×) | Copilot gpt-5.3-codex (6×) only as a third-lineage read on a named high-risk slice | medium | Copilot | 18× cheaper per prompt. ''Spend the remaining November allowance before 11-01 00:00Z.'' It expires unused. |

Standing rules the roster implies (REASONED):
* `gpt-6-sol` leaves every launch line, the k17-r brief's trailer and resume commands.
* No Codex seat runs `--ephemeral`. Luna included: its reads have never been counted.
* One fresh Codex seat per slice beats one long resumed seat (cached re-reads dominate).
* spend.py gains four things: credits per Codex seat (the §1.1 table), pool-% first/last per rollout, the controller's
main session, and the 5m/1h write split.

!!! Three experiments for this week

''E1. Subagent cache TTL (Claude).'' Set `subagentPromptCacheTtl: "1h"` (operator's settings change) for one week.
* Before: spend.py for the week of 10-26 shows Sonnet subagents at 91.5M cache-write vs 809M read (ratio 0.113), with
250 re-writes after a 5–60 min gap.
* After: the ratio should fall below ≈0.03, with re-write count near 0. The Claude `/usage` weekly % per lane-day
should drop.
* Confirm on the plan meter: read the Claude weekly % before and after one comparable lane-day. If writes fall but the
weekly % does not, the plan does not weight cache writes the way the API does. Revert, and say so in the swarm canon.

''E2. Builder A/B (Codex).'' Run two comparable slices, Terra-6.1 medium against Sol-6.1 low, each in a fresh seat with
the same brief head.
* spend.py (with credits) shows credits and pool-% per seat.
* The review record shows Luna-high and Episkopos findings per slice, HIGH/MEDIUM counts, fix commits after the read
(rework), and red-before-fix compliance.
* Decision rule: credits per accepted slice, including rework seats. Terra wins only if it lands at least as clean at
≤60% of Sol-low's credits.

''E3. Reader recall (Codex).'' On 3–4 slices, at least two of them security-class, run Luna-high and Sol-6.1-high
read-only on the same diff. Run them blind to each other and to the author's rationale.
* The controller adjudicates each finding as confirmed, false or duplicate.
* The review records show confirmed findings unique to each reader and false positives.
* spend.py shows credits per confirmed finding.
* If Luna-high misses a confirmed HIGH that Sol catches on a security-class slice, the deep-reader rule in the table
stands as law. If Luna matches, Sol returns to named questions only.

